WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.680
Picture two cells that touch.

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.800
One carries a B-type ephrin, held in its membrane.

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.940
The other carries its receptor.

4
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:17.200
When the two proteins bind, the receptor signals into its own cell.

5
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.320
That is the forward signal.

6
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.700
A second message passes into the ephrin's cell.

7
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.580
That is reverse signaling.

8
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.120
Now suppose you delete the gene for the ephrin, and an organ fails to form.

9
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:31.720
What did you take away?

10
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:36.930
You have removed the forward signal into the receptor's cell.

11
00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.490
You have also removed the reverse signal into the ephrin's cell.

12
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:46.730
And you have removed the plain grip that held the two cells together.

13
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.700
Deleting the receptor has the same problem.

14
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.960
Its outer part was a ligand for the other cell.

15
00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.520
So here too, one deletion cannot say which loss mattered.

16
00:01:00.417 --> 00:01:04.637
The knockout cannot tell you which loss caused the defect.

17
00:01:05.337 --> 00:01:07.597
The failed organ does not say which.

18
00:01:08.417 --> 00:01:10.777
So here is a design problem for you.

19
00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:16.157
How could we disrupt reverse signaling while leaving forward signaling intact?

20
00:01:17.257 --> 00:01:21.137
Design the mouse, or the reagent, for an ephrin of the B class.
